WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.333
Here is a result that feels wrong.

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:05.033
A team nearly doubles how much it can build.

00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:08.333
But the work that people accept and use does not grow.

00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:10.966
More building did not create more outcomes.

00:00:10.966 --> 00:00:12.866
We explore why in Leverage Lab.

00:00:14.066 --> 00:00:17.766
Work moves through six stages, from shape to adopt.

00:00:17.766 --> 00:00:21.600
The slowest stage is the constraint, the bottleneck.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.433
Work can leave only as fast as it passes that stage.

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:29.266
Speed up any other stage, and nothing more gets out.

00:00:29.266 --> 00:00:32.666
Work just piles up in front of the bottleneck.

00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:35.700
Here, about 8 new items arrive each week.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.066
Build can pass 19.

00:00:37.066 --> 00:00:38.966
Verify can pass only 5.7.

00:00:38.966 --> 00:00:41.866
So Verify sets the pace for everything.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.433
To find the constraint, you need telemetry.

00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:48.633
Telemetry is data about how work moves through each stage.

00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:51.366
Without it, you can only see which stage is loud.

00:00:51.366 --> 00:00:55.433
Here, telemetry covers 95 percent of Build, but only 32 percent of Verify.

00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:58.200
Build has the biggest queue and the most activity.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.533
So the evidence points at Build, with 71 percent confidence.

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:04.733
It is wrong, because the data is missing where it matters.

00:01:05.966 --> 00:01:07.900
Suppose you trust that evidence.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.333
Every cycle, you add more agents and a delivery partner.

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:15.933
Over four six-week cycles, accepted outcomes fall from 5.2 to 1.3 per week.

00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:18.666
Work in progress grows from 64 items to 148.

00:01:18.666 --> 00:01:20.900
More building made the system worse.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.300
Now spend at the real constraint.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.000
In cycle one, automate verification and limit new work.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.033
Verify gets faster, and arrivals drop to 6.5.

00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:35.400
Now Adopt is the slowest stage, so the constraint moves to Adopt.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.400
Output rises to 6.5 per week.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.900
In cycle two, fund adoption and make handoffs clear.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.200
Arrivals drop to 5, and every stage can now pass more than that.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.633
The backlog drains, and output reaches 10 per week.

00:01:49.866 --> 00:01:51.500
But a backlog runs out.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:57.366
In the next cycles, output falls back to 4.7 per week, even if you change nothing.

00:01:57.366 --> 00:02:01.833
The pipeline can pass about 9 items a week, but only 5 arrive.

00:02:01.833 --> 00:02:04.533
The constraint has left the pipeline.

00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:07.700
Now the limit is how much new work comes in.

00:02:08.866 --> 00:02:10.100
The constraint moves.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.066
Find it again after every change.

00:02:12.066 --> 00:02:13.700
Try it yourself in the lab.
